   "title": "Do not shorten the server's Retry-After delay",
   "severity": "P1",
   "location": "retrying-transport.ts:79",
   "failure": "Intent Drift: capping Retry-After contradicts the requirement to honor it. Through createIngestClient and a real local HTTP server, a 503 with Retry-After: 12 caused the next GET after 10,001 ms, before the requested 12 seconds. A two-minute instruction likewise becomes ten seconds with the default policy. This retries while the service has requested no further traffic and can exhaust the retry budget before recovery. Retry-After semantics: https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Headers/Retry-After.",
   "fix": "Apply maxDelayMs only to client-generated backoff. Wait at least the server-requested interval, or stop retrying if that interval exceeds an explicit operation budget. Correct the capped Retry-After assertion in retrying-transport.test.ts:67-71."
  },
  {
   "title": "Retry recoverable HTTP 500 responses",
   "severity": "P1",
   "location": "retrying-transport.ts:32",
   "failure": "Intent Drift: the status allowlist omits 500, so temporary internal server errors bypass the required retry behavior. A real local server configured to return 500 with Retry-After: 1 and then 200 received only one GET through createIngestClient; stationName threw IngestError immediately. The same allowlist applies to PUT uploads.",
   "fix": "Include HTTP 500 among retryable statuses and extend an existing response-retry test with a 500 followed by success. Keep permanent server errors such as 501 outside the retry set."
  },
  {
   "title": "Interpret HTTP dates in UTC instead of the host timezone",
   "severity": "P2",
   "location": "retrying-transport.ts:77",
   "failure": "Boundary correctness: Date.parse is not an HTTP-date parser. RFC 9110 requires recipients to accept the asctime form and interpret it as UTC. With TZ=Europe/Berlin and now=2026-03-01T12:00:00Z, a real HTTP response carrying Retry-After: Sun Mar  1 12:00:05 2026 produced a zero-ms wait instead of 5,000 ms. Date.parse interpreted it as 11:00:05Z. The same parser also accepted invalid Retry-After values -1 and 1.5 as old dates, producing zero instead of the tested backoff fallback. HTTP-date contract: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110.html#section-5.6.7.",
   "fix": "Parse the supported HTTP-date formats with UTC semantics and reject non-HTTP date strings. Extend the existing date and malformed-header tests with a non-UTC timezone and numeric-looking invalid values."
  },
  {
   "title": "Cover POST rejection in the send-once test",
   "severity": "P2",
   "location": "retrying-transport.test.ts:46-51",
   "failure": "Missing behavioral test: the only POST test covers a resolved 503, not a rejected network request. A regression that excludes POST only after receiving a response could pass this test while retrying failed alert requests through the separate catch path. Each POST creates a new alert, so this unprotected requirement prevents duplicate side effects. Current production behavior is correct: a real socket-reset probe made one POST connection and performed no retry sleeps.",
   "fix": "Extend the existing POST test with a rejecting transport case. Assert the original error is propagated, the transport is called once, and no retry sleep occurs."
  }
 ],
